  "query_preview": "..Moist southerly flow aloft for much of the next \n7 days will keep anomalously high PWAT values for September \nacross the area. This will lead to a daily threat of isolated to\nwidely scattered flash flooding with some of the strongest \nstorms producing rainfall rates of 1-2\"/hr, possibly higher,  \nmainly during the afternoon and evening hours. Model guidances \nare hinting that Monday and Wednesday will see the greatest \nthreat of scattered heavy rain and flash flooding due to the \npotential for upper level disturbances moving across the area. \nThursday also looks interesting as models are bringing the \nremnants of a tropical upper low, which is still developing off \nthe Mexican coast this morning well S-SE of the tip of Baja, \nnorth across NW Mexico on the western flank of the sub-tropical \nridge with the high centered over Louisiana. Confidence low on\ntiming of this feature this far out. WPC is highlighting the \neastern portions of the forecast area Monday, Wednesday and \nThursday for excessive rainfall. The potential wet week ahead \nmay lead to some enhanced flows in washes, creeks, and rivers \nacross the forecast area that are already saturated. High \ntemperatures this week will show daily variations from near \nnormal to several degrees below normal.",
